{ Capablanca, a world champion, wrote a famous book on endings, and these are his lessons. Here you are a pawn down: hold the draw. King to d5, facing his. [%cal Ye5d5] } 2... Kd5 { The opposition: kings face each other with one square between, and the side to move must give way. It is his move. [%csl Gd5,Rd3,Bd4] } (2... Kd6 { Back to d6, and the squares in front of his king are left free. } 3. Kd4 Ke7 4. Ke5 Kf7 5. Kd6 { Lost: his king stands on d6, ahead of its own pawn, and yours can no longer block the road. [%csl Rd6] }) (2... Ke6 { King to e6 steps back instead of facing him. } 3. Ke4 Kf6 4. Kf4 Ke6 5. Ke4 { Lost: his king faces yours from e4 with one square between, and now you are the one who must give way. [%csl Re4,Ge6] }) (2... Kf5 { King to f5 leaves the d column open for him. } 3. Kd4 Kf6 4. Kd5 Ke7 5. Ke5 { Lost: his king got to e5, in front of his pawn, the one square you had to keep. [%csl Re5] }) (2... Kf6 { King to f6 walks away from the square in front of him. } 3. Kd4 Kg6 4. Kc4 Kf7 5. Kd5 { Lost: his king walked up to d5, and nothing of yours stands between it and the road for the pawn. [%csl Rd5] }) 3. e4+ { His pawn checks. Step back to e5 and stay in front of it. [%cal Yd5e5] } 3... Ke5 { Back on e5, right in front of the pawn. Now watch him try to push it through. [%cal Gd5e5][%tail] } (3... Kc6 { King to c6 also holds, one step further from the pawn. } 4. Ke3 { Drawn all the same: e5 is still two steps away for your king, and his pawn has not moved yet. [%csl Ge5] }) (3... Ke6 { King to e6 is a draw too, one row further back. } 4. Ke3 { Still drawn: your king on e6 blocks the road of the pawn, just from further away. [%csl Ge6] }) (3... Kc5 { King to c5 also draws, but it stands beside the pawn instead of in front. } 4. Ke3 { A draw, though a risky one: from c5 your king has to hurry back in front of the pawn before it moves. [%csl Rc5] }) (3... Kd6 { King to d6 draws as well. } 4. Kd4 { Still a draw: kings face each other on d4 and d6, and your king can step to e6 in front of the pawn. [%csl Gd6,Re6] }) 4. Ke3 Ke6 5. Kd4 Kd6 6. e5+ Ke6 7. Ke4 Ke7 8. Kd5 Kd7 9. e6+ Ke8 10. Kd6 Kd8 11. e7+ Ke8 { Your king stayed in front all the way to e8. His king can only guard the pawn from e6, and that is stalemate: a draw. [%csl Ge8,Re6] } 0-1


