{ Now use the opposition to win. Your pawn stands on e5, and his king waits on e8. } 8. Ke6 { The opposition: your king on e6 faces his on e8 with one square between, and he must give way. [%csl Ge6,Re8,Be7] } (8. e6 { The pawn goes first and leaves your king behind it. } 8... Kf8 9. Kg6 Ke8 10. Kf5 Ke7 { Drawn: his king sits on e7, right in front of the pawn, and yours never got ahead of it. [%csl Re7] }) (8. Kf5 { King to f5 steps back from the pawn instead of in front of it. } 8... Kf7 9. Kg4 Kg6 10. Kf4 Kf7 { Drawn: his king on f7 guards e6 and e7, the squares the pawn must cross, and your king cannot chase it off. [%csl Re6,Re7] }) (8. Kg6 { King to g6 goes one column too far, past the road of the pawn. } 8... Ke7 9. Kg5 Ke6 10. Kf4 Kd7 { Drawn: his king got in front of the pawn first, and yours had to run back to f4 just to keep it. [%csl Rd7] }) (8. Kg5 { King to g5 drops a row behind the pawn. } 8... Kf7 9. Kf4 Kg6 10. Kg4 Kg7 { Drawn: his king stays close enough to reach e7 in front of the pawn, and yours has drifted off to g4. [%csl Re7] }) 8... Kf8 { His king gives way to f8. } 9. Kd7 { Your king takes d7, beside the queening square on e8, and the pawn walks up behind it. [%tail][%cal Ge6d7][%csl Ge8] } (9. Kf6 { King to f6 also wins: it still stands in front of the pawn. } 9... Ke8 { Still winning: his king comes back to face yours, and you take one more step to get past. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (9. Kd6 { King to d6 wins too, one move slower. } 9... Kf7 { Also winning: d6 is still in front of the pawn, but your king has to step up to d7 once more. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (9. Kd5 { King to d5 drops back behind the pawn. } 9... Ke7 10. Ke4 Kf7 11. Ke3 Ke6 { Drawn: his king took e6, in front of the pawn, and your king ended up behind it. [%csl Re6] }) (9. Kf5 { King to f5 steps back and gives him f7. } 9... Kf7 10. Kg4 Kg6 11. Kf4 Kf7 { Drawn: his king holds f7 and e7, and the pawn on e5 never gets past. [%csl Rf7] }) 9... Kf7 10. e6+ Kf6 11. e7 Ke5 12. e8=Q+ { A new queen on e8, with check. Your king took the opposition and went first, and his king never got in front of the pawn. [%csl Ge8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Keep the distant opposition"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/4p1p1/8/5P2/6K1/3k4_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
