[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/8/4p1p1/8/5P2/6K1/3k4 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E15"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Opposition"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 28, critical-position extract"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]

{ You are a pawn down, and his king is coming for your pawn on f3. Hold the draw. } 1. Kh1 { Kings on h1 and d1, three squares between them on the same row: distant opposition, and he is the one who must move. [%csl Gh1,Rd1,Be1,Bf1,Bg1] } (1. Kh3 { King to h3 leaves the first row, where the count of squares mattered. } 1... Ke1 2. Kg3 Kf1 3. Kg4 Ke2 { Lost: his king reached e2, next to your pawn on f3, and your king on g4 is the one giving way. [%csl Rf3] }) (1. Kf1 { King to f1 goes straight at him, and your own pawn will block the next step. } 1... Kd2 2. Kg1 Kd3 3. Kf1 Ke3 { Lost: his king on e3 attacks the pawn on f3, and your king on f1 cannot reach it. [%csl Rf3] }) (1. Kf2 { King to f2 guards the pawn, but hands him the opposition. } 1... Kd2 2. Kf1 Ke3 3. Kg2 Ke2 { Lost: his king has walked all the way to e2, deep in your half, and yours is pushed back to g2. [%csl Re2] }) (1. Kg3 { King to g3 steps up a move too soon. } 1... Ke1 2. Kh3 Kf2 3. Kg4 Kg2 { Lost: his king slipped round to g2, behind your king and your pawn. [%csl Rg2] }) 1... Kd2 { His king steps up to d2. } 2. Kh2 { Kings on h2 and d2: three squares between on the second row, and he must move again. [%csl Gh2,Rd2] } (2. Kg1 { King to g1 breaks the count of three. } 2... Kd3 3. Kf1 Ke3 4. Kg2 Ke2 { Lost: his king walked up to e2, and yours on g2 is the one that has to give way. [%csl Re2] }) (2. Kg2 { King to g2 leaves the h column and breaks the count. } 2... Ke2 3. Kg3 Kd1 4. Kh3 Ke1 { Lost: his king stands on e1, next to f2, and your king on h3 is too far from the pawn on f3. [%csl Rf2] }) (2. f4 { The pawn runs to f4 instead of the king moving. } 2... exf4 3. Kh2 Ke2 4. Kh3 Kf3 { Lost: his king on f3 stands beside his f pawn, and yours is shut in on h3. [%csl Rf3] }) 2... Kd3 { His king steps up to d3. } 3. Kh3 { Kings on h3 and d3, three squares apart on the third row, and still he must move. [%csl Gh3,Rd3] } (3. Kg3 { King to g3 breaks the count and hands him the opposition. } 3... Ke3 4. Kg2 Ke2 5. Kg3 Kf1 { Lost: his king got to f1, behind your pawn, and yours cannot shake it off. [%csl Rf1] }) (3. Kg1 { King to g1 steps back and sideways at once. } 3... Kc3 4. Kg2 Kc2 5. Kg3 Kd1 { Lost: his king has walked round to d1, behind your pawns, while yours went nowhere. [%csl Rd1] }) (3. Kh1 { King back to h1 loses a row. } 3... Kc3 4. Kg2 Kc2 5. Kg3 Kd1 { Lost: you stepped back to h1, the count broke, and his king came round to d1 by the back row. [%csl Rd1] }) (3. Kg2 { King to g2 leaves the h column. } 3... Ke2 4. Kg3 Kd1 5. Kf2 Kd2 { Lost: his king on d2 faces yours on f2 across e2, and it is you who must give way. [%csl Rd2,Gf2] }) 3... e4 { He throws his e pawn at your pawn on f3. } 4. fxe4 { Your pawn takes on e4, and the pawns start coming off. [%tail][%cal Gf3e4] } (4. f4 { You push past him instead of taking. } 4... gxf4 5. Kg2 e3 6. Kf1 f3 { Lost: his pawns on e3 and f3 have his king beside them on d3, and you cannot stop both. [%csl Re3,Rf3] }) (4. Kg2 { King to g2 leaves his pawn on e4 alive. } 4... e3 5. Kf1 e2+ 6. Kf2 Kd2 { Lost: the pawn on e2 has his king beside it on d2, and nothing of yours can touch it. [%csl Re2] }) (4. Kg4 { King to g4 goes after the g pawn, a move you do not have. } 4... e3 5. Kg3 e2 6. Kf2 Kd2 { Lost: his king on d2 walks the e pawn in, and your king on f2 arrived one move late. [%csl Re2] }) 4... Kxe4 5. Kg4 Ke5 6. Kxg5 { Your king takes the last pawn on g5. Two bare kings: drawn. The count of three squares kept his king out the whole way. [%csl Gg5] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Spend a reserve pawn move"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/4k3/8/4K3/8/4P3/8_w_-_-_2_2"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/3rgJ8AAv"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/4k3/8/4K3/8/4P3/8 w - - 2 2"]
[ChapterId "E01"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Spare move"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 6, critical-position extract"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ A tempo is a spare move: it changes nothing except whose turn it is. Your pawn has one. The kings face off on e4 and e6, so push to e3 and make him move. [%cal Ye2e3][%csl Ge4,Re6] } 2. e3 { Your pawn spent the tempo, and now he is the one who must give way. [%cal Ge2e3] } (2. Kf4 { King to f4 also wins, and the pawn move is still saved. } 2... Kf6 { Still winning: kings on f4 and f6, and your tempo on e2 is still in hand. [%cal Be6f6] }) (2. Kd4 { King to d4 wins too, keeping the pawn move for later. } 2... Kd6 { Also winning: kings on d4 and d6, and you can spend the tempo whenever the kings face off. [%cal Be6d6] }) (2. Kf3 { King back to f3 gives up the square in front of him. } 2... Kf5 3. Ke3 Ke5 4. Kf3 Kf5 { Drawn: his king stands in front on f5 and e5 every move, and yours never gets past. [%csl Rf5] }) (2. Ke3 { King to e3 stands in front of your own pawn, so the pawn cannot move at all. } 2... Ke5 3. Kf3 Kf5 4. Ke3 Ke5 { Drawn: with the pawn stuck behind your king there is no tempo to spend, and he faces you every move. [%csl Re5] }) 2... Kf6 { He gives way to f6. Walk past him: king to d5. [%cal Ye4d5] } 3. Kd5 { Your king slips past his and takes d5, and the pawn waits on e3. [%cal Ge4d5] } (3. Kd4 { King to d4 wins too, a little slower. } 3... Ke6 { Still winning: he faces you on e6, but your pawn still has its one-step tempo to e4. [%cal Bf6e6] }) (3. Kf4 { King to f4 is also a win. } 3... Kg7 { Winning as well: his king drifted to g7, and yours has the road to e5. [%cal Bf6g7] }) (3. Kd3 { King to d3 steps back instead of forward. } 3... Ke5 4. Kd2 Kf6 5. Ke2 Ke6 { Drawn: you gave back d4 and e4, and his king now sits in front of your pawn. [%csl Re6] }) (3. Kf3 { King to f3 wanders away from d5 and e5. } 3... Ke7 4. Ke4 Ke6 5. Kf4 Kf6 { Drawn: his king faces yours on e6 and f6, and the pawn on e3 has no second tempo to spend. [%csl Rf6] }) 3... Ke7 { He hurries back to block. Stand right in front of him: king to e5. [%cal Yd5e5] } 4. Ke5 { The opposition: kings on e5 and e7 with one square between, and he must give way. [%csl Ge5,Re7] } (4. e4 { The pawn charges to e4 while your king is not yet in front of it. } 4... Kd7 5. e5 Ke7 6. e6 { Drawn: his king parks on e7 in front of the pawn, and your king on d5 can never shift it. [%csl Re7] }) (4. Kc5 { King to c5 strays from the road of the pawn. } 4... Ke6 5. e4 Ke5 6. Kb4 { Drawn: his king stands on e5, square in front of your pawn, and holds it there. [%csl Re5] }) (4. Kd4 { King back to d4 gives up the square your king just won. } 4... Kd6 5. e4 Ke6 6. e5 { Drawn: his king holds e6 in front of your pawn, and your king never gets ahead of it. [%csl Re6] }) (4. Ke4 { King to e4 steps back behind its own pawn. } 4... Ke6 5. Kd3 Kd5 6. e4+ { Drawn: your pawn checks, his king steps back to e5 or e6, and your king has gained nothing. [%csl Rd5] }) 4... Kd7 { He gives way to d7. Go round the other side: king to f6. [%cal Ye5f6] } 5. Kf6 { Round the other side, and your king is ahead of the pawn. Now watch it walk home. [%cal Ge5f6][%tail] } (5. e4 { The pawn charges to e4 before your king is in front of it. } 5... Ke7 6. Kd4 Kd6 7. e5+ { Drawn: your pawn checks from in front of your king, and his king settles in its path on e6. [%csl Re6] }) (5. Kd5 { King to d5 also wins, but it is the long way round. } 5... Ke7 6. Ke5 Kd7 7. Kf6 { Still winning: you reach f6 in the end, two moves later than the direct road. [%cal Ge5f6] }) (5. Kf5 { King to f5 wins as well, by a slower road. } 5... Ke7 { Also winning: his king holds e7, and you need one more step to f6 to get past. [%cal Bf5f6] }) (5. Kf4 { King to f4 wins too, but it drops back a row for nothing. } 5... Ke6 6. Ke4 Kd6 7. Kf5 { Still winning, though your king went back to f4 and had to come forward again to f5. [%cal Bf4f5] }) 5... Kc6 6. e4 Kd7 7. e5 Ke8 8. Ke6 Kd8 9. Kf7 Kc7 10. e6 Kb6 11. e7 Kc5 12. e8=Q { A new queen on e8. One tempo at the start, and your king led the pawn home. [%csl Ge8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: One square, not two"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/6p1/8/8/7p/7k/7P/6K1_b_-_-_1_8"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/qtmFvp4T"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/6p1/8/8/7p/7k/7P/6K1 b - - 1 8"]
[ChapterId "E05"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Spare move"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 23, critical position with the white king on g1 so that the single step is the winner"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ You are Black. Your g pawn can step one square or two, and only one of them wins. Count the race before you push. } 8... g6 { One step only. The second step stays in your pocket as a tempo, for the moment it is worth something. [%cal Gg7g6] } (8... g5 { Two squares at once looks quicker. Watch what his king does in the corner. } 9. Kh1 g4 10. Kg1 g3 11. hxg3 hxg3 12. Kh1 g2+ 13. Kg1 Kg3 { Stalemate! Your pawn on g2 takes f1 and h1, your king on g3 covers the rest, and he has no move. Drawn. [%csl Rg1] }) (8... Kg4 { Your king goes hunting on g4 while both pawns stand still. } 9. Kg2 h3+ 10. Kf1 g6 11. Ke1 g5 { Drawn: his king walked out to e1 while yours was busy, and from there it stops both pawns. [%csl Re1] }) 9. Kh1 { His king sits in the corner, beside the pawn on h2 that it has to guard. } 9... g5 { Now the step you saved. His king has to move, and the corner leaves it only g1. [%cal Gg6g5] } (9... Kg4 { Your king steps out to g4, and his king escapes the corner with it. } 10. Kg2 h3+ 11. Kf1 g5 12. Ke1 { Drawn: his king is out of the corner and home on e1, in time to stop both of your pawns. [%csl Re1] }) 10. Kg1 { His king has one square, g1: your king on h3 covers g2. [%csl Rg2] } 10... g4 { The pawn keeps coming, and his king has to go back to the corner. [%cal Gg5g4] } (10... Kg4 { Your king steps out to g4 and lets his king out of the trap. } 11. Kg2 h3+ 12. Kf1 Kf3 13. Kg1 { Drawn: his king is out of the corner for good, and your pawns on g5 and h3 have stopped moving. [%csl Rg1] }) 11. Kh1 { Back to h1. He shuttles between two squares while your pawn marches. } 11... g3 { Now he has to take: the pawn on h2 cannot move and his king cannot leave it. [%cal Gg4g3] } 12. hxg3 { He takes on g3. Which of your pawns takes back? } 12... hxg3 { The h pawn takes, and your king on h3 stands right beside the new pawn on g3. [%cal Gh4g3] } (12... Kxg3 { The king takes on g3, and your last runner is a lone rook pawn. } 13. Kg1 h3 14. Kh1 h2 { Stalemate: his king in the corner in front of your rook pawn has no move. A rook pawn with his king in front of it is a draw. [%csl Rh1] }) 13. Kg1 { His king is back on g1, the one square left to it. } 13... g2 { The pawn steps to g2 and takes f1 and h1 away from him. [%cal Gg3g2] } (13... Kg4 { Your king wanders to g4 and leaves the pawn with no guard. } 14. Kg2 Kf4 15. Kg1 g2 16. Kxg2 { Drawn: his king collects the pawn on g2, and two bare kings are left. [%csl Rg2] }) 14. Kf2 { He is pushed out to f2. The pawn or the king: which goes first? } 14... Kh2 { Your king guards the pawn on g2, and nothing can touch it now. [%tail][%cal Gh3h2] } (14... g1=Q+ { A new queen on g1, with check! Look at the square she lands on. } 15. Kxg1 { Drawn: his king on f2 was standing right beside g1, and it takes the new queen at once. [%csl Rg1] }) 15. Ke1 g1=Q+ { A new queen on g1, with check. The single step at the start kept a tempo in reserve, and that spare move won the race. [%csl Gg1] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca endings: Improve the king before capturing"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/4p3/6p1/5P2/8/3k3K_w_-_-_0_2"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/j7omKLhM"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/8/4p3/6p1/5P2/8/3k3K w - - 0 2"]
[ChapterId "E16"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Spare move"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 28, critical-position extract"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]

{ You are a pawn down, and his g pawn on g4 is there for the taking. Hold the draw. } 2. Kg2 { Your king steps up to g2 before taking. It guards the pawn on f3, and it is one step nearer to his e pawn. [%cal Gh1g2][%csl Gf3] } (2. f4 { The pawn runs to f4 and gives up the fight for g4. } 2... exf4 3. Kh2 g3+ 4. Kh3 Kd2 { Lost: his pawns on f4 and g3 are running, and your king on h3 has to stay beside the g pawn. [%csl Rf4,Rg3] }) (2. fxg4 { A free pawn, taken at once. Count the race first. } 2... e4 3. Kg2 e3 4. Kf3 Kd2 { Lost: his king on d2 guards the pawn on e3, and it queens before your pawn on g4 gets going. [%csl Re3] }) (2. Kg1 { King to g1 steps sideways, not forward. } 2... gxf3 3. Kf2 e4 4. Ke3 Ke1 { Lost: his pawns on e4 and f3 stand side by side, and your king cannot stop both. [%csl Re4,Rf3] }) (2. Kh2 { King to h2 is one step further from the fight. } 2... gxf3 3. Kg3 Ke2 4. Kg4 e4 { Lost: his king on e2 guards f3, and his e pawn on e4 has a free road. [%csl Re4,Rf3] }) 2... Kd2 { His king steps away from the g pawn and toward his e pawn. } 3. fxg4 { Now take it: your king on g2 is close enough. Both sides have a runner. [%cal Gf3g4] } (3. Kf2 { King to f2 heads for his e pawn and leaves g4 alone. } 3... gxf3 4. Kxf3 Kd3 5. Kf2 e4 { Lost: his e pawn has his king beside it on d3, and yours on f2 has no pawn left to race with. [%csl Re4] }) (3. Kg3 { King to g3 goes to take the g pawn with the king instead. } 3... gxf3 4. Kxf3 Kd3 5. Kf2 e4 { Lost: your pawn was taken on f3, so you have no runner, and his e pawn marches with its king on d3. [%csl Rd3] }) (3. f4 { The pawn runs to f4 instead of taking. } 3... exf4 4. Kf2 Kc1 5. Ke2 Kc2 { Lost: his pawns on f4 and g4 are two against none, and your king cannot hold both. [%csl Rf4,Rg4] }) (3. Kg1 { King to g1 steps back just when the race starts. } 3... gxf3 4. Kf2 e4 5. Kg3 Ke2 { Lost: his king on e2 guards the pawn on f3, and his e pawn has a clear road. [%csl Re2] }) 3... e4 { His pawn sets off down the e column. } 4. g5 { Your pawn runs at once: a king step now would cost the one move this race needs. [%tail][%cal Gg4g5] } (4. Kf2 { King to f2 chases his e pawn directly. } 4... e3+ 5. Kf3 e2 { Lost: the pawn on e2 is one step from a queen, your king cannot reach it, and your own pawn never started. [%csl Re2] }) (4. Kg3 { A king step instead of a pawn step. } 4... e3 5. Kf3 e2 { Lost: his e pawn is about to queen while your pawn still sits on g4. [%csl Re2] }) 4... e3 5. g6 e2 6. g7 e1=Q 7. g8=Q { Both pawns became queens, yours on g8 and his on e1, and neither side can win that: a draw. The king step before the capture was the tempo the race needed. [%csl Gg8,Re1] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Race along the right column"]
