{ The king leads: in an ending your king is a fighting piece, so it walks up first and the pawns advance beside it. Bring it to e5. [%cal Yd4e5] } 2. Ke5 { Your king stands on e5, right beside the pawns it will escort. [%cal Gd4e5] } (2. g5 { The pawn to g5 also wins: the h pawn guards it. } 2... Kd6 { Still winning: his king went to d6, and your king can still come up to lead. [%cal Be7d6] }) (2. Kd3 { King to d3 wins too, by a longer road. } 2... Kd6 { Still winning, just slower: your king has to come back up to d4 and start again. [%cal Be7d6] }) (2. Kd5 { King to d5 wins as well. } 2... Kf6 { Still winning: his king comes to f6, but yours on d5 can still get to e4 and lead. [%cal Be7f6] }) (2. Ke4 { King to e4 is another winning square, a row further back. } 2... Kd6 { Still winning: your king on e4 is one row back, but close enough to lead the pawns. [%cal Be7d6] }) (2. h5 { The h pawn runs ahead, alone. } 2... Kf6 3. h6 gxh6 { Drawn: the pawns swapped on h6, and his king on f6 stands guard over what is left. [%csl Rf6] }) (2. f6+ { The f pawn checks, far ahead of your king. } 2... Kxf6 { Drawn: his king took your f pawn, and it is two pawns against two. [%csl Rf6] }) 2... Kf7 { His king stays on f7 to guard his pawns. Push the g pawn to g5. [%cal Yg4g5] } 3. g5 { The g pawn steps up to g5, guarded by the h pawn and watched by your king. [%cal Gg4g5] } (3. h5 { The h pawn to h5 also wins: the g pawn guards it. } 3... Ke7 { Still winning: your pawn on h5 is safe, and your king still leads from e5. [%cal Bf7e7] }) (3. f6 { The f pawn runs past your king. } 3... gxf6+ 4. Kd4 f5 5. gxf5 Kf6 6. h5 Kxf5 { Drawn: his king took on f5, and his h pawn blocks yours, one pawn each. [%csl Rf5] }) (3. Kd6 { King to d6 walks off and leaves the pawns alone. } 3... g6 4. fxg6+ Kxg6 5. g5 h6 6. gxh6 Kxh6 { Drawn: his king on h6 stands in front of your last pawn on h4, and a rook pawn cannot get past that. [%csl Rh6] }) 3... Ke7 { His king steps to e7, facing yours. Bring the h pawn up to h5. [%cal Yh4h5] } 4. h5 { The h pawn joins the others, and your king on e5 still guards the chain. Now watch the pawns roll. [%cal Gh4h5][%tail] } (4. g6 { The g pawn runs ahead of the others. } 4... hxg6 5. fxg6 Kd7 6. h5 Ke7 7. h6 gxh6 { Drawn: his pawn took on h6, and his king on e7 is one step from f7, in front of your last pawn. [%csl Rf7] }) (4. f6+ { The f pawn checks and runs ahead of the others. } 4... gxf6+ 5. gxf6+ Kf7 { Drawn: his king blocks your f pawn on f7, and his h pawn on h7 stops yours. [%csl Rf7] }) (4. Kd5 { King to d5 still wins. } 4... Kd7 { Still winning, though your king stepped away from the pawns it was leading. [%cal Be7d7] }) (4. Kd4 { King back to d4 also wins, but it leaves the pawns on their own. } 4... Kd6 { Still winning, though your king has to climb back to e5 before the pawns go on. [%cal Be7d6] }) (4. Ke4 { King to e4 also wins, a row behind the pawns. } 4... Kf7 { Still winning: from e4 your king can step straight back up beside the pawns. [%cal Be7f7] }) (4. Kf4 { King to f4 wins as well. } 4... Kd6 { Still winning, but your king went back behind the pawns instead of beside them. [%cal Be7d6] }) 4... Kf7 5. g6+ { The g pawn checks, and whichever way he answers, one of your pawns gets through. } 5... Ke7 6. gxh7 Kd7 7. h8=Q { A new queen on h8. Your king stood on e5 the whole time, and the pawns walked up beside it: the king leads. [%csl Gh8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Walk around the pawn wall"]
