{ His king guards his lone pawn on g7, and your pawns on f5 and g6 look stuck. Win it. } 1. Kd7 { Your king heads round the wall by d7, on the way to e7. [%cal Ge6d7] } (1. Kd6 { King to d6 also wins. } 1... Ke8 { Still winning: from d6 your king can still reach d7 or e7 round the wall. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (1. Kd5 { King to d5 wins too, more slowly. } 1... Ke7 { Still winning, but his king got to e7 first, so yours takes the long way round. [%cal Bf8e7] }) (1. Ke5 { King to e5 is another winning road. } 1... Ke7 { Winning as well: from e5 your king still has the road round by d6. [%cal Bf8e7] }) (1. f6 { The pawn breaks before your king is round the wall. } 1... Kg8 2. fxg7 Kxg7 3. Kd5 Kxg6 { Drawn: the pawns swapped off, and two bare kings cannot win. [%csl Rg6] }) 1... Kg8 { His king steps back to g8. } 2. Ke7 { Your king reaches e7, behind the wall, where it guards f7 and f8. [%cal Gd7e7][%csl Gf7,Gf8] } (2. Ke8 { King to e8 is as good as e7 for getting behind the wall. } 2... Kh8 { Still winning: from e8 your king guards f7 and f8 just the same. [%csl Gf7,Gf8] }) (2. Kd6 { King to d6 steps back a square. } 2... Kf8 { Still winning, a move slower: d6 is further from f7 than e7 is. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kd8 { King to d8 also wins. } 2... Kh8 { Also winning: d8 is one step from e7 and e8. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (2. Ke6 { King back to e6, where it started. } 2... Kf8 { Still winning, but you have spent two moves to stand where you began. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kc6 { King to c6 walks away from the pawns. } 2... Kh8 { Winning, though c6 is three steps from f7, and the king must come back. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (2. Kc7 { King to c7 is a detour. } 2... Kf8 { Still winning: c7 is a side road, and e7 is where the king is going. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kc8 { King to c8 takes the longest road. } 2... Kh8 { Also winning, by the slowest route: from c8 your king needs three moves to reach f7. [%cal Bg8h8] }) 2... Kh8 { His king is in the corner on h8, with g8 its only way out. } 3. f6 { Your pawn jabs at f6, right where his g pawn can take it, and the wall comes apart. [%cal Gf5f6] } (3. Kf7 { Careful! Your king on f7 takes g8 from him, his g pawn is blocked, and he has no move at all: stalemate, a draw. [%csl Rh8] }) (3. Kf8 { Your king on f8 takes g8 away too, and with his g pawn stuck he cannot move: stalemate, drawn. [%csl Rh8] }) (3. Ke6 { King back to e6 before the break. } 3... Kg8 { Still winning, but the wall is untouched and his king walked out to g8. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd6 { King to d6 waits a move. } 3... Kg8 { Also winning, a move slower: from d6 your king must come back before the break. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd7 { King to d7 waits instead of breaking. } 3... Kg8 { Winning all the same, but d7 gave him a free move to step out to g8. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd8 { King to d8 does nothing to the wall. } 3... Kg8 { Still a win, though his king walked out to g8 while your pawns stood still. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Ke8 { King to e8 asks him nothing. } 3... Kg8 { Also winning, but your pawns on f5 and g6 are exactly where they were. [%cal Bh8g8] }) 3... gxf6 { His g pawn takes on f6. } 4. Kf7 { Your king takes g8 away from him, and the g pawn has a clear road to queen. [%cal Ge7f7][%csl Gg8] } (4. Kxf6 { Taking the pawn also wins, one move slower. } 4... Kg8 { Still winning: your king on f6 guards g7, and the pawn walks on from there. [%csl Gg7] }) (4. Kf8 { King to f8 also wins. } 4... f5 { Still winning: his f pawn runs, but your g pawn is closer to a queen. [%cal Bf6f5] }) (4. g7+ { A check, and the pawn is one square from a queen. Look again. } 4... Kxg7 { Lost: his king takes on g7, your last pawn is gone, and his f pawn is free to run. [%csl Rg7] }) 4... f5 { His f pawn runs. } 5. g7+ { The pawn checks on g7, guarded by your king on f7. [%tail][%cal Gg6g7] } (5. Kf8 { King to f8 also wins, more slowly. } 5... f4 { Still winning: your king still guards g7 and g8, and your pawn is further up than his. [%csl Gg7,Gg8] }) (5. Kf6 { King to f6 goes after his pawn and lets his king out. } 5... Kg8 6. Kxf5 Kg7 { Drawn: his king on g7 stands in front of your pawn on g6 and takes it next. [%csl Rg7] }) 5... Kh7 6. g8=Q+ { A new queen on g8, with check. Your king went round the wall, and the pawn break did the rest. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


