{ His king on f8 blocks your pawns on f6 and g5, and his own pawn sits on g6. Find the way in. } 8. f7 { The pawn gives itself up on f7. Once it is gone, f6 is an empty square your king can use, right beside the g6 pawn. [%cal Gf6f7][%csl Gf6] } (8. Ke6 { King to e6 also wins, more slowly. } 8... Ke8 { Still winning: his king holds the back row, but yours still finds a way in. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (8. Kd5 { King to d5 wins by a longer road. } 8... Kf7 { Still winning: he steps up to guard your pawn's square, and your king has further to walk. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (8. Kd6 { King to d6 wins too. } 8... Kf7 { Also winning: his king blocks the f pawn, and your king looks for another door. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (8. Kd4 { King back to d4 keeps the win, at the cost of time. } 8... Ke8 { Still winning, but your king has three steps to come back before anything happens. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (8. Ke4 { King to e4 wins as well, a move behind. } 8... Ke8 { Winning, though your king stepped back and the pawns on f6 and g5 did nothing. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (8. Kf4 { King to f4 also keeps the win. } 8... Ke8 { Still winning, by a roundabout road: from f4 your king has no quick way past his. [%cal Bf8e8] }) 8... Kxf7 { He takes the pawn on f7. } 9. Kd6 { Your king heads round by d6, toward e6 and e7. [%cal Ge5d6] } (9. Kd5 { King to d5 also wins. } 9... Ke7 { Still winning, a move slower: his king takes e7, and yours must go round by d6. [%cal Bf7e7] }) (9. Kf4 { King to f4 goes the wrong way round. } 9... Kf8 10. Ke5 Ke7 11. Kd5 Kd7 { Drawn: his king faces yours on d7 with one square between, and you cannot get round. [%csl Rd7,Gd5] }) (9. Kd4 { King back to d4 gives up e5. } 9... Ke6 10. Ke4 Kd6 11. Kd4 Ke6 { Drawn: his king faces yours on every move, and the ground the pawn sacrifice won is gone. [%csl Re6] }) (9. Ke4 { King to e4 steps back. } 9... Ke6 10. Kf4 Kd5 11. Kg4 Ke5 { Drawn: his king comes to e5, next to f6, and guards the way to g6. [%csl Re5,Rf6] }) 9... Kf8 { His king steps back to f8. } 10. Ke6 { Your king takes e6, beside f6 and f7: the squares his king has left are yours now. [%tail][%cal Gd6e6] } (10. Kd7 { King to d7 also wins. } 10... Kf7 11. Kd6 { Still winning, but slower: his king got back to f7, and yours has to come round again. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (10. Kd5 { King to d5 wins too, a step behind. } 10... Ke7 11. Ke5 { Still winning, but his king reached e7, so yours needed a move to get back to e5. [%cal Bf8e7] }) (10. Ke5 { King to e5 walks straight into his opposition. } 10... Ke7 { Drawn: his king on e7 faces yours on e5 with one square between, and your king never gets to f6. [%csl Re7,Ge5] }) (10. Kc6 { King to c6 goes the wrong way. } 10... Ke7 11. Kd5 Kd7 { Drawn: his king on d7 faces yours on d5, and there is no way round. [%csl Rd7] }) 10... Kg7 11. Ke7 Kg8 12. Kf6 Kh7 13. Kf7 Kh8 14. Kxg6 Kg8 15. Kh6 Kf7 16. g6+ Kf8 17. Kh7 Ke7 18. g7 Kd6 19. g8=Q { A new queen on g8. Your king walked in through f6 and f7, the squares his king had left, and stepped to h7 so his king never reached g8. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


