{ An active rook works from a distance: it checks, attacks, or guards from the side, never waiting in front of a pawn. You are two pawns down: hold the draw. Rook to d6. [%cal Yd3d6] } 1... Rd6 { Your rook on d6 is on its way to d8, where it can guard f8 from the side. [%cal Gd3d6,Gd6d8,Bd8f8] } (1... Rd5 { Rook to d5 also holds. } 2. Rb7 { Drawn as well: from d5 your rook still reaches d8 in time. [%cal Bh7b7] }) (1... Kd6 { King to d6 heads for the pawn, and the rook stays passive. } 2. Rb7 Ke6 3. f7 Rd8 4. Kg3 Kf6 { Lost: his rook on b7 guards the pawn on f7 from the side, and your b pawn on b4 has no defender. [%csl Rb4] }) (1... Rd8 { Rook straight to d8 looks quicker. } 2. Rb7 Kd6 3. Rxb4 Ke6 4. Rf4 { Lost: his rook took your b pawn and now guards f6 from f4, and he is three pawns up. [%cal Rf4f6] }) (1... Kc6 { King to c6 leaves the rook to do everything alone. } 2. Re7 Rd5 3. Re4 b3 4. h4 { Lost: his rook on e4 can stop your b pawn, and his f pawn on f6 has a free road to f8. [%csl Rf6] }) (1... Rd4 { Rook to d4 sits one row too low. } 2. Rb7 Rg4+ 3. Kf3 Rg6 4. f7 { Lost: his pawn reached f7, his rook on b7 attacks your pawn on b4, and your rook on g6 is too late for f8. [%cal Rb7b4][%csl Rf7] }) (1... Rd2 { Rook to d2 goes after pawns instead of the runner. } 2. Rb7 Rd8 3. f7 Kd6 4. h4 { Lost: his rook on b7 guards f7 from the side and watches your b pawn at the same time. [%cal Rb7f7] }) 2. f7 { His pawn steps to f7. Rook to d8, and guard the queening square from the side. [%cal Yd6d8] } 2... Rd8 { Your rook on d8 guards f8 along the back row. Now watch his tries. [%cal Gd6d8,Gd8f8][%tail] } (2... Rf6 { Rook to f6 also draws: it stands behind the pawn and attacks it. } 3. Rg7 { Drawn as well: your rook on f6 takes the pawn the moment its guard leaves. [%cal Bh7g7] }) (2... Rg6+ { The check looks active, but it takes the rook away from d8. } 3. Kf1 Rf6 4. Ke2 Kd6 5. Rh6 { Lost: his rook on h6 pins yours on f6 to your king on d6, and the pawn on f7 queens. [%cal Rh6d6] }) (2... Kd5 { King to d5 is a move too slow. } 3. f8=Q Rg6+ 4. Kh1 Rd6 5. Rh5+ { Lost: the pawn queened on f8 while your rook stood on d6, and he has a queen against your rook. [%csl Rf8] }) (2... Kc6 { King to c6 leaves f8 unguarded. } 3. f8=Q Rg6+ 4. Kf3 Rd6 5. Qa8+ { Lost: his new queen came out of f8 and now checks your king from a8. [%csl Ra8] }) 3. Rh5+ Kc4 4. Rh4+ Kc3 5. Kg3 Rf8 { Your rook drops onto f8, in front of the pawn, and the pawn can never queen. [%csl Gf8] } 6. Rf4 b3 { His rook guards the pawn on f7, yours holds f8, and your b pawn runs. His checks gained nothing: a draw. [%csl Gf8,Gb3] } 0-1


