{ You are two pawns down, and his rook checks your king from c7. Hold the draw. } 2... Kd4 { Your king steps to d4, close to your b pawn and on the way to his f pawn. [%cal Gc5d4] } (2... Kd5 { King to d5 also holds. } 3. f7 { Drawn as well: from d5 your king stays close to both the b pawn and his f pawn. [%cal Bf6f7] }) (2... Kb6 { King to b6 backs away from everything. } 3. f7 Rg6+ 4. Kf1 Rf6 5. Rc4 { Lost: his rook on c4 stops your b pawn, and your king on b6 is too far to help it. [%csl Rb4] }) (2... Kb5 { King to b5 runs to the edge. } 3. Rb7+ Kc5 4. f7 Rg6+ 5. Kf3 { Lost: the check on b7 drove your king back, and his pawn reached f7 with time to spare. [%csl Rf7] }) (2... Rc6 { Rook to c6 offers the trade. } 3. Rxc6+ Kxc6 4. f7 Kb5 5. Kg3 { Lost: the rooks came off, and nothing of yours can stop the pawn on f7. [%csl Rf7] }) 3. f7 { His pawn steps to f7. } 3... Rg6+ { Your rook checks from the side and gains a move before it gets behind the pawn. [%cal Gd6g6][%tail] } (3... Rf6 { Rook to f6 also holds: it attacks the pawn from behind. } 4. Rb7 { Also a draw: his rook left c7, and your rook on f6 is ready to take on f7. [%cal Bc7b7] }) (3... Rd8 { Rook to d8 looks natural, but his rook is on the seventh row. } 4. Rd7+ Rxd7 5. f8=Q { Lost: his check on d7 pulled your rook off the back row, and the pawn queened on f8. [%csl Rf8] }) (3... Ke5 { King to e5 lets the pawn go. } 4. f8=Q Re6 5. Qg7+ Kd5 6. Qd7+ { Lost: the pawn queened on f8, and his new queen is chasing your king. [%csl Rd7] }) (3... Rd5 { Rook to d5 does nothing about f8. } 4. f8=Q Ke4 5. Qf6 Re5 6. Rd7 { Lost: his pawn queened on f8, and you have a rook against queen and rook. [%csl Rf6] }) 4. Kf1 Rf6 5. Rd7+ Ke5 6. Re7+ Kd4 { Your rook on f6 holds the pawn on f7, your king is back on d4 beside the b pawn, and his checks gained nothing. An active rook and king: drawn. [%csl Gf6,Gd4] } 0-1


