{ Your a pawn on a5 is free to run, and his king on g6 is far away. Win it. } 49. Ra3 { Your rook steps behind the a pawn, to a3. However far the pawn walks, the rook still guards it from behind. [%tail][%cal Gc3a3,Ga3a5] } (49. Rg3+ { The check also wins. } 49... Kf5 { Still winning: his king is driven to f5 first, and your rook can come back to the a column. [%cal Bg6f5] }) (49. Re3 { Rook to e3 wins too, more slowly. } 49... Ra4 { Still winning, but his rook got to a4, in front of your pawn, and yours on e3 is not behind it. [%csl Ra4] }) (49. Rc6 { Rook to c6 attacks his pawn instead of backing yours. } 49... Kf5 50. Rc7 Ra4 51. Rf7+ Kg6 { Drawn: his rook reached a4, in front of your pawn, and your rook on f7 does nothing for it. [%csl Ra4] }) (49. Rc8 { Rook to c8 goes to the back row, far from the pawn. } 49... e5 50. Kh3 Rd4 51. g4 Ra4 { Drawn: his rook sits on a4 in front of your pawn, and your rook on c8 cannot push it past. [%csl Ra4] }) (49. Rc4 { Rook to c4 steps onto a square his rook guards. } 49... Rxc4 50. Kg3 Kf5 51. a6 Ra4 { Lost: his rook took yours on c4 for free, and now it sits behind your pawn on a6. [%csl Rc4] }) 49... Kf5 50. a6 Rb8 51. a7 { Your pawn stands on a7 with your rook behind it on a3. His rook on b8 has to guard a8 for ever, while yours is free: that is the win. [%csl Ga7][%cal Ga3a7,Rb8a8] } 1-0
